Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of important porous, crystalline materials composed of metal ions (clusters) and organic ligands. Owing to the unique redox chemistry, photochemical and electrical property, and catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+\prime+}$ , copper-based MOFs (Cu-MOFs) have been recently and extensively explored in various biomedical fields. In this review, we first make a brief introduction to the synthesis of Cu-MOFs and their composites, and highlight the recent synthetic strategies of two most studied representatives, three-dimensional HKUST-1 and two-dimensional Cu-TCPP. The recent advances of Cu-MOFs in the applications of cancer treat­ ment, bacterial inhibition, biosensing, biocatalysis, and wound healing are summarized and discussed. Furthermore, we propose a prospect of the future development of Cu-MOFs in biomedical fields and beyond.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a type of inorganic-organic hybrid materials with porous, reticular and crystalline structures [1]. They are organized by coordination effects of metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligands. The unique coordination geometry of metal and ligands dictate their different periodic structures and porosities. A lot of metals and organic ligands could be used to engineer MOFs with distinct properties for a wide range of applications including gas separation [2], gas storage/adsorption [3,4], catalysis [5], drug delivery [6–8], bio­ imaging [9], phototherapy [10], and so on. Nanoscale MOFs possess high surface areas and provide many accessible sites with surrounding substances, making it absorbing in diverse biomedical fields [11].
Copper-based MOFs have excellent physicochemical properties among MOFs and have recently attracted wide attentions in various biomedical applications. The standard redox potential of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ is $0.153\mathrm{~eV}$ and it could be influenced by the donor atoms and coordina­ tion geometry, which makes copper redox chemistry of great interest [12]. For example, $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ in Cu-MOFs can oxidize glutathione (GSH), an important antioxidant molecule in cancer cells, to deplete GSH contents. The reduced ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ can further react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in cancer cells to generate cancericidal OH through Fenton-like reactions. Some Cu-MOFs display fascinating enzyme-like catalytic activity, making them attractive in catalytic tumor therapy and biosensing. In addition, Cu-MOFs have moderate magnetic properties and good photothermal conversion abil­ ity, dictating their potential applications in magnetic resonance imaging and photothermal therapy.
Cu-MOFs are attracting increasing attentions with more recognition of biological roles of copper. Copper is a mineral nutrient and partici­ pates in a variety of biological progresses including respiration, metabolism, cell signaling and so on [13,14]. Recent studies have wit­ nessed the understanding of its biological functions from the active site metabolic cofactor to the signaling metal and metalloallosteric regulator [15–17]. Meanwhile, copper homeostasis is fundamental to health of organisms; too high or too low copper contents are intertwined with various diseases. The aberrant copper levels and metabolisms at lesion sites are identified as a promising target for treatment of disorders like cancer [18–20]. Furthermore, some copper-based organic complexes are intrinsically bioactive [21,22], which suggests a great potential of bioactive Cu-MOFs prepared by biological ligands. Overall, these bio­ logical properties of copper allow Cu-MOFs a robust platform toward different biomedical aims [23].
Although there are many available reviews on MOFs and their biomedical applications, a comprehensive summary focusing on CuMOFs and their biomedical advances is very few. To the best of our knowledge, there is only one reported review on the nanomedicine application of Cu-MOFs, where Sun et al. summarized the synthetic methods and applications of nanoscale Cu(II)/Cu(I)-MOFs in antibac­ terial, anti-inflammatory, and anticancer therapy [24]. In this review, we first introduce the controllable synthesis of Cu-MOFs with different dimensions and their composites. Thereafter, we briefly discuss the toxicity concerns of Cu-MOFs and make a summary of recent advances in biomedical applications including anticancer, antibacterial, bio­ sensing, biocatalysis, and wound healing (Scheme 1).
## 2. Synthesis of Cu-MOFs and their composites
## 2.1. Synthesis of MOFs
The typical synthetic methods of MOFs include solvothermal method [25–29], sonochemical method [30,31], mechanochemistry [32,33], microwave synthesis [34], continuous flow production [35], layer-bylayer growth [36], and so on (Fig. 1). Solvothermal method is a tradi­ tional method for facile synthesis of MOFs but is time-consuming and needs harsh conditions like high temperature and high pressure [37]. In contrast, microwave synthesis is time-saving by accelerating the nucle­ ation process. A reasonable selection of synthetic method is needed according to the concrete biomedical applications. For the in vivo ap­ plications, potential toxicity from organic solvents like DMSO, DMF, and methanol should be taken into consideration.
The size and morphology of nanomaterials are important in their physiochemical properties and thereby the biological functions. For example, the uniform size of nanoMOFs is vital for its applications in drug delivery and bio-detection, where the size distributions could affect the biodistribution of drug and the linearly quantitative analysis. The high surface energy of nanoMOFs leads to the ease of aggregation into larger and irregular sizes. Therefore, in order to prepare nanoscale MOFs, a typical method is to use small molecular modulator to compete with organic linkers to bind metal ions, which modulated the important factors (reaction rate and crystalline morphology) in the nucleation and growth processes [11,38–40].
## 2.2. Synthesis of HKUST-1 and Cu-TCPP MOFs
According to the differences in morphology, Cu-MOFs can be cate­ gorized into 1 dimensional (1D), 2D, and 3D MOFs. 1D Cu-MOFs with nanowire and nanorod morphology can be prepared by bottom-up strategy [41,42], liquid exfoliation strategy [43], adsorption method [44], and coordination modulation method [45]. However, their biomedical applications are rarely reported and thus not further intro­ duced. In contrast, 2D and 3D Cu-MOFs are extensively studied in various biomedical fields. HKUST-1 (ligand: 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid, denoted as $\mathrm{H_{3}B T C)}$ and Cu-TCPP (ligand: tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)porphyrin, denoted as TCPP) MOFs are the most widely explored three- and two-dimensional Cu-MOFs, respectively. In this section, we would highlight the recent advances in the controllable synthesis of HKUST-1 and Cu-TCPP.
HKUST-1 (also known as MOF-199), first reported by Chui et al. in 1999 [46], is composed of dimeric cupric tetracarboxylate units (Fig. 2A and B), where each copper ion coordinates with four O atoms from two
BTC molecules and an axial aqua ligand opposite to the Cu-Cu vector (the internuclear separation of Cu-Cu was $\bar{2.628\mathring{\mathrm{A}}}\bar{.}$ ). The HKUST-1 was successfully prepared by a typical solvothermal method at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 h [46]. By using proper precipitation solvents, Zhuang et al. shortened the preparation time to less than 10 minutes by adding the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ solution in DMSO into precipitating solvents (methanol or ethanol) [47]. Another issue in solvothermal synthesis is that some impurities such as $\mathtt{C u_{2}O}$ was generated. To obtain phase-pure MOFs, Wee et al. developed facile pre-cooling and freeze-drying methods to synthesize HKUST-1 in the size range of $100\mathrm{nm}$ to $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , where the size can be tuned by the volume ratios of ethanol and water [48]. However, the procedure is a little complicated and the nucleation and crystal growth process are still not well modulated.
In 2018, Wang et al. proposed a facile and straightforward synthetic strategy by well-tailored nucleation and growth using a syringe pump to precisely control the addition rates of metal ions and ligands (Fig. 2C and D) [49]. The faster feed rate at the beginning of reaction led to a smaller size of HKUST-1. By separately tuning the nucleation and growth, HKUST-1s with uniform and precisely tunable nanosizes ranging from 24 to $567\mathrm{nm}$ and narrow size distributions were obtained. More importantly, this general strategy could be expanded to control­ lable, scale-up preparation of other MOFs like MIL-100(Fe), ZIF-8, MOF801, etc. For example, MIL-101(Fe) and UIO-66(Zr) at a $_{5\mathrm{~L~}}$ scale were fulfilled by replacing syringe pump with a peristaltic pump.
In 2019, a liquid-solid-solution (LSS) method was proposed by Cai et al. to synthesize highly uniform nanoscale HKUST-1, whose growth takes place at the interfaces between ethanol-n-hexane-oleic acid liquid phase and water-ethanol solution (Fig. 3A) [50]. The nucleation speed was significantly reduced by the copper oleate clusters and the OA protection. By using the LSS method, HKUST-1 in a relatively narrow size range (30 to $140~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) were synthesized. The size of HKUST-1 was controlled by the adding amounts of OA; larger size MOFs were obtained at a higher OA feeding amount. Noteworthily, this method is more environmentally friendly because of the DMF-free synthesis.
Different from the typical three-dimensional HKUST-1, twodimensional $\mathrm{Cu(HBTC)}{\cdot}1$ was prepared by using the in situ synthe­ sized $\mathtt{C u_{2}O}$ cubic and $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ as metal nodes and organic ligands, respectively (Fig. 3B) [51]. In the 2D MOFs, two carboxylate groups of $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ involved with the coordination with cupric ions. Furthermore, 2D Fe-BTC nanosheets with peroxidase-like activity can be prepared by using a cation substitution method.
Next, we would like to highlight the synthesis of two-dimensional Cu-TCPP MOFs that have an ultra-small thickness of about $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the intrinsic photo-sensitiveness. Cu-TCPP MOFs with different sizes have been designed and synthesized mainly by a PVP-assisted solvothermal method, where PVP facilitates the anisotropic growth of MOFs into 2D structures. In 2018, Li et al. prepared $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCPP MOFs with a size of $330\mathrm{nm}$ and a thickness of $5.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ (DMF and PVP. $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $24\mathrm{h}$ . Fig. 4B) [52]. The ultrathin Cu-MOFs exhibited near-infrared absorption and could be used for photothermal therapy (PTT) of cancer. In another study by Wang et al., Cu-TCPP with a size of $106\ \mathrm{nm}\times37\ \mathrm{nm}$ was synthesized similarly (a mixed solvent of DMF and n-hexane, PVP and $\mathrm{CF_{3}C O O H}$ . $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 2 h. Fig. 4A) [53]. In this study, the authors found that cancericidal $^10_{2}$ could be generated through a Russell mechanism by harnessing the intracellular glutathione (GSH) and hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , avoiding the use of light irradiation that is needed in typical photodynamic therapy (PDT) [54–58]. Similarly, bigger Cu-TCPP(Fe) NSs with an average hydrodynamic size of $645~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and a thickness of $3{-}5\mathrm{nm}$ were prepared for antibacterial application (Fig. 4C), where the nanosheets showed a peroxidase activity to generate OH from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [59].
In 2020, Zhao et al. further pushed the size limit of Cu-TCPP to a micron-size using benzoic acid (BA) as the modulator, which promoted the coordination of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and porphyrin, reduced H-stacking and J-ag­ gregation between the layers, and caused anisotropic growth of the MOF sheet [60]. The as-prepared Cu-TCPP(BA) displayed a micron size range of $3{-}8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and a thickness of $1{-}2~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4D). Noteworthily, a signif­ icant difference of the Cu-TCPP(BA) to the Cu-TCPP is that ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{u}}^{2+}$ did coordinate the central ring of TCPP, which was proved by a remarkable Q-band absorption at $540\mathrm{nm}$ in the UV-vis absorption and the decrease of pyrrolic nitrogen at $400.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the XPS spectrum [60]. In another words, $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ is not only located at the central ring of TCPP but also at the linking sites of adjacent TCPP molecules. This micron-sized MOF sheets could have promising applications in catalysis and optoelectronic devices.
## 2.3. Synthesis of Cu-MOFs composites
Cu-MOFs alone are difficult to meet the complicated needs in biomedical applications. For example, the relatively inferior stability in physiological conditions makes them hard to be directly used for cancer treatment; the low electroconductivity of most Cu-MOFs limits its direct use in highly efficient electrochemical analysis [40,61]. Therefore, composites of Cu-MOF and other materials, polymer for example, are usually fabricated to combine their advantages [62,63].
First, the reactive groups $\boldsymbol{(\mathrm{NH_{2}}}$ or COOH) on the surface of Cu-MOFs dictate that they can be chemically modified with other materials to construct composites with enhanced physiological stability or other biological functions. For example, a typical EDC/NHS method could be employed to conjugate HKUST-1 to polymers or proteins (like enzymes [64] or streptavidin [65]), where amide bonds are formed by the reac­ tion of the carboxylic groups on the surface of MOF and the primary amine groups in polymers or proteins.
In the context of drug delivery, the metal ions and aromatic ligands provide abundant interaction sites with drugs by various forces including coordination interactions [66], π–π stacking [67] and hydro­ phobic interactions. Generally, there are two methods to prepare drugloaded MOFs. First, drugs can be in situ loaded during MOF formation [68,69]. Alternatively, drugs can be soaked into a MOF suspension for a period of time and the drug-incorporated MOFs are obtained by centrifugation [70,71]. In the latter case, the drug molecular size should be less than the pore size of MOFs so that drugs can diffuse into the internal spaces. Furthermore, similar interactions between the surface of MOF and polymers could further realize surface modification for enhanced blood circulation and tumor targeting ability.
MOFs/polymer composite is a fascinating combination that integrates the advantages of both materials, for example the porosity and processability [72]. MOFs in polymer hydrogel is a star composite owing to the many merits of hydrogels including good biocompatibility, biodegradability, flexibility and the broad range of biomedical appli­ cations [73]. The introduction of MOFs could improve the mechanical property of hydrogels [74,75]. MOFs/hydrogel composites can be syn­ thesized by direct mixing method (MOFs synthesis first) or in situ MOF synthesis (gelation first), which are recently reviewed elsewhere [73]. In addition, MOF-embedded polymer fibers and membranes could be facilely prepared by electrospinning technology using a simple mixture of MOF suspension and polymer solution [76–79].
## 3. Biomedical applications of Cu-MOFs
Before the discussion of biomedical applications, we would like to first pay attention to the toxicity of Cu-MOFs, which is one of the main concerns of MOFs as biomaterials. The toxicity of MOFs is dictated by the building metal ions and ligands but also by the particle size, morphology, agglomeration and stability [80–82]. The toxicities of HKUST-1 against cells[83], zebrafish embryos [84,85], and mice [86] were mostly studied. Ruyra et al. compared the cytotoxicity of a library of MOFs and found that HKUST-1 displayed higher cytotoxicity than other MOFs like ZIF-8 and MIL-100 against two cancer cell lines HepG2 and MCF-7 [85]. In the context of zebrafish, HKUST-1 with a size of $50{-}100~\mathrm{nm}$ showed an IC50 value of 2.13 and $1.50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ for zebrafish
