## 3.1. Anticancer
Before the introduction of the detailed anticancer advances, we would like to first give an outline of the copper in cancer and cancer treatment, functional ligands, and using Cu-MOFs as carriers of common anticancer drugs.
Copper is essential in the tumorigenesis [87], progression, and metastasis because copper is not only important as cofactors in various enzymes but also an important signaling metal, both of which are highly needed in the uncontrolled proliferation of malignant cells [17,19]. On the other hand, the redox property of copper dictates its overload can kill cancer cells by generating cytotoxic ROS. Therefore, there are two current copper-based strategies for cancer treatment: (1) copper deple­ tion by chelators to reduce the bioavailability of copper [88–90]; (2) copper over-supply to disrupt the copper and redox homeostasis in cancer cells [91–93]. In the framework of Cu-MOFs, obviously, the latter strategy is adopted; the underlying anticancer mechanisms are the wellknown Fenton-like reactions or Harber-Weiss reactions (Fig. 5B) [19], where the redox chemistry of copper ions played a key role. Further­ more, like anticancer platinum-based complexes [94–96], many copperorganic complexes are excellent anticancer drugs by targeting inhibition of proteins or DNAs [21,97] ; a recent study reveals that some of these copper complexes can induce cancer cell death by cuproptosis [98]. Furthermore, the physical photoelectrical property of copper can further render nanoMOF the potentials in cancer imaging (PAI, MRI, and PET) and phototherapy.
Because of the porous structure and high drug loading efficiency, CuMOFs have been widely used for delivering various anticancer drugs including 5-fluoracil [99], ibuprofen [100], amoxicillin [101], metho­ trexate [102], capecitabine [103], doxorubicin [67,104,105], cisplatin [66], photosensitizers [106], rifampicin [107] and so on. Like other MOFs, these bioactive molecules can be incorporated into Cu-MOFs by different methods including one-pot synthesis, biomimetic mineraliza­ tion, post-synthetic pore encapsulation, and so on [108]. The drug loading is governed by the physical properties of Cu-MOFs and the carrier-drug interactions. For example, HKUST-1 showed a larger pore size of $15.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ than MIL-100(Fe) $(7.{\dot{6}}{\dot{\mathrm{\AA}}})$ and thereby a selective loading of rifampicin with a size of $11.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ [107]. The supramolecular in­ teractions between Cu-MOFs and payloads, such as $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking in­ teractions and van der Waals forces, dictate the drug loading efficiency and stability. High drug loading contents of $73.7\%$ ibuprofen and $80\%$ rifampicin were obtained in Cu-TPA and HKUST-1, respectively [107,109]. In addition, nanoscale Cu-MOFs composing of copper ${{(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{u}}^{2+}}$ or ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}.$ ) alone or with other transition metal ions (like $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ [110], $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ [111], $\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}[112])$ and organic ligands are designed and synthesized for cancer treatment owing to the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect of nanomedicines. Further surface modification by syn­ thetic or natural polymers (like PVP, PEG, HA, etc.) could render the enhanced physiological stability in blood circulation prior to the arrival at tumor sites [113].
Next, we would like to introduce the recent advances of Cu-MOFs in cancer treatment from four aspects (Fig. 5A): (1) Cu redox chemistry (Fig. 5B); (2) imaging and photothermal conversion; (3) anticancer Cuorganic complexes delivery; and (4) functional ligands.
## 3.1.1. Cu redox chemistry
${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ as an oxidant can react with intracellular antioxidants that usually have higher concentrations in cancer cells than normal cells. Glutathione is a typical antioxidant and can reach a high concentration of up to $10~\mathrm{mM}$ in cancer cells. [114,115] ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ can scavenge intracel­ lular GSH by oxidizing GSH to GSSG [116]. The GSH depleting property of Cu-MOFs is alluring in improving the antitumor effect of PDT and chemotherapy since that GSH can neutralize the cancericidal effect of $^10_{2}$ generated in PDT and some anticancer drugs like cisplatin by forming cisplatin-GSH conjugate [117]. Therefore, Cu-MOFs could be used to construct GSH-depleting and/or responsive drug delivery sys­ tems for enhanced PDT efficacy [118,119] and reversing chemotherapy resistance. For example, Wang et al. developed a photosensitizer (TPAAQ)-loaded HKUST-1 for GSH-activated TPAAQ release and PDT in cancer cells (Fig. 6A) [118]. The HKUST-1 could scavenge intracellular GSH, which led to disintegration of nanoMOF. Upon light irradiation, the TPAAQ@HKUST-1 nanoparticles (NPs) displayed a selective inhi­ bition of HepG2 cancer cells over NIH-3T3 cells due to the higher GSH concentrations in cancer cells. Similarly, Zhang et al. developed GSHabsorbing CuL- $(\mathrm{AlOH})_{2}$ (ligand: mesotetrakis(4-carboxylphenyl) porphyrin) MOFs for enhanced PDT by reducing GSH levels and syner­ gistically improving reactive oxygen species (ROS) concentrations. The Cu-MOFs displayed comparable antitumor effect to commercial anti­ cancer agent camptothecin (CPT) in a mouse tumor model [120].
Furthermore, GSH consumption can induce cancer cell death in a manner of ferroptosis, where GSH depletion causes inactivation of glutathione peroxidase 4 (GPX4), an enzyme responsible for the reduction of toxic lipid peroxide to noncytotoxic lipid hydroxyl com­ pounds [70,122]. He et al. reported a Fe/Cu hybrid MOF with a disulfide bond containing ligand for multimodal imaging (ultrasound imaging and MRI) and GSH depletion-induced ferroptosis [110]. $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , and GSH-responsive linkers collectively led to significant GSH depletion, thereby inactivating GPX4 and inducing ferroptosis.
Besides of GSH consumption, Ni et al. recently found that ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ in MOFs can oxidize estrogen for cancer therapy (Fig. 6C) [121]. In their study, estradiol-loaded Cu-MOFs (ligand: 5,10,15,20-tetrabenzoatopor­ phyrin, $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}{\mathrm{:}}$ displayed a nanoplate morphology and a pHresponsive disassociation and liberation of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and photosensitizer TBP. The released estradiol was oxidized by cytochrome P450 to obtain 4-hydroxy estradiol, which further subsequently with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to generate the oxygen radicals $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ and $\phantom{+}0_{2}^{-}$ (Fig. 6D and E). Upon irradi­ ation of $650~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light, the photosensitive ligand can generate $^10_{2}$ for PDT. These ROS-induced acute local inflammation is highly immuno­ genic [123]. Therefore, the combination of Cu-MOFs-mediated CDT and PDT with a programmed cell death ligand 1 (PDL1) blockade effectively inhibited the primary and distant tumor growth in an estradiol over­ expressing B16F10 bilateral mouse model (Fig. 6F).
Upon scavenging intracellular GSH, ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ is reduced to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ that could further react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in cancer cells (up to $50{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [124,125]) to produce •OH through a Fenton-like reaction [126–128]. The highly cytotoxic •OH irreversibly reacts with nearby biomolecules (DNA, proteins, lipids) to kill cancer cells and fulfills chemodynamic therapy (CDT) [129,130]. Although the OH producing ability make CuMOFs are excellent candidates for CDT, the CDT potency is primarily dependent on the copper and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations at lesion sites. Since tumor is heterogenetic [131], it is reasonable to augment intratumoral $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations by delivering glucose oxidase (GOx) [132] or small molecules [133,134] that can upregulate intracellular ROS level to tumor sites. For example, cisplatin is a typical anticancer drug and it can induce ROS elevation by activation of NADPH oxidase (NOXs). Xiang et al. developed Cu-BDC MOFs for efficient loading of $\mathrm{Pt(IV)}$ prodrug and tumor inhibition by chemotherapy-assisted oxidative stress (Fig. 7A) [133]. The prodrug could be reduced to $\mathrm{Pt(II)}$ drug that is able to activate NOXs and elevate intracellular $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations for enhanced CDT. In addition to augment the copper and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concen­ trations, advanced nanotechnology such as single-site nanozyme is also a feasible means to improve the CDT effect. In 2021, Wang et al. developed a Cu-hexacyanoferrate (Cu-HCF) nanozyme that acts as a tumor-specific glutathione oxidase and the reduced $\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ could undergo a Fenton-type Harber-Weiss reaction to generate •OH for cancer cell in­ hibition [135].
Another redox chemistry that Cu-MOF involved with is the genera­ tion of $^10_{2}$ by Russell mechanism [53]. In 2019, Wang et al. reported that Cu-TCPP nanosheets were able to deplete GSH and generate $^10_{2}$ by a Russell mechanism, overcoming the limitations of PDT where oxygen and light irradiation are needed and the effect is usually hampered by the neutralization of $^10_{2}$ by GSH (Fig. 7B) [53].
## 3.1.2. Imaging and photothermal conversion
The unique electromagnetic property of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ make copper excellent electroconducting and thermocondcucting materials. The un­ paired 3d electrons of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ make it possible for MR imaging [52]. The d–d energy band transition of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ in Cu-organic (like TCPP) [52] or inorganic (chalcogen [136]) complexes dictate that they are able to absorb light of interested wavelength and transfer the light energy to heat energy, suggesting its potential of near-infrared thermal imaging and vibration that could be detected by photoacoustic imaging. In 2018, Li et al. synthesized ultrathin $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCPP nanosheets with a size of $330\mathrm{nm}$ and a thickness of $5.1\mathrm{nm}\pm0.3\mathrm{nm}$ by a solvothermal method [52]. The Cu-TCPP NSs can absorb near infrared light and display an excellent photothermal conversion efficiency of $36.8\%$ . Furthermore, Cu-TCPP fulfilled MR $(\mathbf{r}_{1}=1/\mathrm{T}_{1}=0.52\mathrm{mM}^{-1}s^{-1})$ and thermal imaging and a combination therapy of PDT and PTT. In a Fe/Cu hybrid MOF, a higher $\mathbf{r}_{1}$ value of $3.87\ \mathrm{mM}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ was obtained, which was also much higher than those of free $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ $(0.76\ m\mathrm{M}^{-1}\ s^{-1})$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ $\left[0.20\mathrm{\mM}^{-1}\mathrm{\bar{\s}}^{-1}\right]$ [110].
$^{64}\mathrm{Cu}$ isotope is a positron emitter with a half-life of $12.7\mathrm{h}$ and the optimal decay property dictates its potential application in positron emission tomography (PET) [139,140], which has advantages of excel­ lent imaging sensitivity and tissue penetration ability. Duan et al. sys­ tematically studied the impact of nanoparticle size on the cellular uptake, drug release, and tumor accumulation of amorphous zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (AZIF-8) [137]. A series of AZIF-8 at a size range of 30 to $130\ \mathrm{nm}$ were synthesized and the PET imaging of chelator-free $^{64}\mathrm{Cu}$ -labled MOFs revealed that $60\ \mathrm{nm\MOF}$ displayed better blood circulation and 1.50 times accumulation at tumor sites than the $130\mathrm{nm}$ counterpart (Fig. 8B).
Interestingly, some Cu-MOFs have intrinsic photothermal effect. In addition to the above-mentioned Cu-TCPP nanosheets, ${\mathrm{Cu/Fe}}$ hybrid MOFs with disulfide-modified phloroglucinol [110] or dithiodiglycolic acid [70] as linkers have been reported to have near-infrared absorption and photothermal conversion upon exposure to $808~\mathrm{nm}$ laser. Such a photothermal conversion was also observed in the copper ferrite nano­ spheres [141]. Cu-MOFs can also be used to synthesize photothermal materials for photoacoustic imaging and PTT. In 2020, Weng and co­ workers used HKUST-1 as a precursor and prepared Cu nanoparticles/ defective porous carbon polyhedron composite by pyrolysis in an argon atmosphere [142]. By tuning the carbonization temperature, nano­ composites with different photothermal effects were obtained. The composite prepared at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ showed the highest PTCE of $48.5\%$ under $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light irradiation. The in vivo PA imaging and ablation of tumor by PTT was demonstrated.
Compared to pre-synthesis of photothermal Cu-MOFs, those can leverage the endogenous stimulus to in situ generate photothermal and PA imaging materials could be more attractive because of the tumorspecificity and the potential in measuring the stimulus level at tumor sites. By leveraging the abundant $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in colon cancers, Zhang et al. developed a ratiometric photoacoustic imaging $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ probe by using the in situ sulfidation of HKUST-1, which possessed excellent PA signal, high selectivity, and rapid kinetics in response to tumoral $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ (Fig. 8C) [138]. The PVP-modified Cu-MOF showed a linear increase in the photoacoustic ratio of PA1250/PA700 in response to increasing NaHS concentrations and the chemical equilibrium was reached within merely $30\ s.$ . Benefiting from the high selectivity and rapid kinetics, the CuMOFs demonstrated efficient $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ detection in subcutaneous and orthotopic colon tumor models, suggesting its potential in monitoring the endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ level in vivo and imaging of colon cancer.
The strong affinity of $s^{2\cdot}$ toward $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ dictate that the endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ could be used to generate photothermal copper sulfide (CuS) nanoparticles in situ for PTT [143,144]. Li et al. employed HKUST-1 as $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -activating photothermal nanosystem by the in situ sulfidation re­ action (Fig. 9A). NaHS treatment of HKUST-1 led to formation of CuS nanoparticles with strong NIR absorption (Fig. 9B) and photothermal effect; the latter was positively relevant to the NaHS concentrations (Fig. 9C). The Cu-MOFs displayed effective treatment of CT26 colorectal cancer by CDT and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ -triggered PTT because of the high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ concen­ trations in colon cancers (Fig. 9D) [145]. Furthermore, the antitumor effect of HKUST-1 was improved or inhibited by a combination with aminooxyacetic acid (AOAA) or S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM), which are activator and inhibitor of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ producing cystathionine-betasynthase, respectively.
## 3.1.3. Delivery or in-situ generation of anticancer Cu-organic complexes
Metal-organic complexes (based on platinum, copper, and others) are an important class of anticancer agents. Copper(II)-complexes with various ligands including 8-hydroxyquinoline [146,147], di­ thiocarbamates (DTCs) [148], etc. have shown broad anticancer activ­ ities. Of these, copper(II) diethyldithiocarbamate (CuET), which is the main product of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and clinical alcoholism drug disulfiram (DSF), has attracted great attentions in cancer therapy [22,149,150]. Nanoscale Cu-MOFs including HKUST-1 [151], Cu-2MI MOF [68], and Sm-TCPP (Cu) nanosheets [152] are excellent reservoirs of copper ions and have been explored for CuET-based chemotherapy. Hou et al. designed HAmodified nanoscale Cu-MOFs (ligand: 2-MI) for CD44-targeting cancer cell uptake and the acidic condition in lysosomes initiated the pHresponsive release of DSF and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ (Fig. 10A) [68]. The in situ chemi­ cal reactions of DSF and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ generated CuET, which killed 4T1 breast cancer cells by binding nuclear protein localization 4 (NPL4) target. In the in vitro degradation study, characteristic UV-vis absorption of CuET at $420~\mathrm{{nm}}$ appeared in the condition of $\mathrm{pH}5.6\$ and $150~\mathrm{U/mL}$ HAase (Fig. 10B), suggesting the pH-responsive and HAase-triggered degra­ dation and CuET formation. A tumor inhibition rate of $86\%$ was obtained in a 4T1 subcutaneous tumor model (Fig. 10C and D).
DSF has high reactivity with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ owing to the strong coordination effects of S and Cu. To avoid the undesired formation of CuET in the preparation and/or delivery processes, our group recently presented a DSF prodrug DQ-loaded and GOx modified HKUST-1 nanosystem (MPDG), in which GOx can catalyze tumoral glucose to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ that could not only trigger prodrug activation and formation of CuET but also boost the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -based Fenton-like reactions (Fig. 10E) [153]. The nano­ complex MPDG demonstrated glucose-responsive generation of CuET (Fig. 10F) and remarkably enhanced cytotoxicity in the presence of 0.5 $\mathrm{mg/mL}$ glucose (Fig. 10G). In vivo antitumor study revealed that MPDG displayed efficient suppression of 4T1 tumor growth with an inhibition rate of $86.2\%$ (Fig. 10H and I). The above systems suggest that Cu-MOFs may be a robust nanoplatform to enhance the chemotherapy effect of Cu-organic compounds.
## 3.1.4. Functional ligands
Generally, ligands like $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ merely facilitate the formation of the porous MOFs but do not have biological functions nor contribute to tumor inhibition. Therefore, functional ligands are widely employed to construct smart Cu-MOFs with an attempt to realize controlled MOFs disintegration and drug release or activatable therapeutic functions by harnessing the ligand’s responsiveness to endogenous (pH, GSH, hyp­ oxia, etc.) or exogenous (light, sonication) stimuli, which is expected to realize tumor-specific therapy and enhanced therapeutic outcomes. In this section, we emphasize the anticancer exploration of smart Cu-MOFs
that are synthesized by pH-, GSH-, hypoxia-, and light-sensitive ligands.   
Some typical examples were summarized in Table 1.
3.1.4.1. pH-sensitive ligands. Acidic microenvironment is a typical hallmark of solid tumors, where the tissues are slight acidic $(\mathrm{pH}6.5\ –7.1)$ [154,155] and the intracellular organelles endosomes and lysosomes are more acidic $\mathrm{(pH~}4.5\mathrm{-}5.5)$ [156,157]. pH-responsive drug delivery sys­ tems, therefore, could realize acidity-accelerated drug release and enhanced anticancer efficacy [158,159]. As for Cu-MOFs, pH-sensitive ligands including 2-methylimidazole (2-MI) [68,126,127], $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}$ [121], terephthalic acid (TPA) [102,109], and $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ [151] have been employed to construct pH-responsive nanosystems. In the copper-MOFs, 2-MI-based MOFs showed pH-responsive release of DOX [127] and DSF [68]. Taking the DOX-loaded MOFs as an example, the in vitro release study manifested that about $75\%$ DOX was released at $^{36\mathrm{~h~}}$ in the condition of $\mathrm{pH}5.0$ while only $15.5\%$ DOX was liberated at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ . The high DOX loading $(17.8\%$ in weight) and the rapid release at endo­ somal/lysosomal pH dictated the enhanced cytotoxicity against HepG2 cells.
3.1.4.2. GSH-sensitive ligands. As an important antioxidant, GSH level in cancer cells could be high up to $10~\mathrm{mM}$ to maintain the redox ho­ meostasis owing to the elevated ROS level [161]. Disulfide ligands are cleavable by GSH and could be used to design GSH-sensitive or scav­ enging MOFs [162]. He et al. utilized a disulfide containing phlor­ oglucinol ligand to prepare Cu-MOFs for GSH-activatable US imaging and ferroptosis (Fig. 11A) [110]. GSH in cancer cells cleaved the di­ sulfide bond in ligands and triggered the release of perfluoropentane (PFP), a US imaging contrast agent. The GSH depletion by ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and ligands together with lipid peroxidation led to ferroptosis of cancer cells. The ${\mathrm{Cu/Fe}}$ hybrid MOFs displayed photothermal conversion ca­ pacity and elevated tumor temperature to $46.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ upon $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ laser treatment $(1.25\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 11B). The in vivo Huh-7 tumor suppressing results (Fig. 11C) suggested that the best tumor inhibition was achieved by the MOFs $^+$ NIR group via a combination of PTT and ferroptosis.
Similarly, dithiodiglycolic acid-based Cu-MOFs were developed by Liang et al. to physically encapsulate anticancer drug DOX [70]. Significantly accelerated release of DOX was observed in the presence of $10~\mathrm{mM}$ GSH. The GSH depletion by ligand and metal ions $(\bar{\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}/\mathsf{F e}^{3+})$
induced the inactivation of GPX4 and subsequent ferroptosis. Combi­ nation with DOX chemotherapy and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ -based Fenton-reaction, the MOFs demonstrated effective chemodynamic/photothermal/ chemotherapy of breast cancer in vitro and in vivo. As we had discussed before, all Cu-MOFs have the GSH depleting ability due to the redox chemistry of copper; the GSH-sensitive linker could be expected to further enhance the GSH-responsiveness and depleting ability of CuMOFs.
3.1.4.3. Hypoxia-sensitive ligands. The hypoxic tumor microenviron­ ment inspires the development of hypoxia-responsive drug release sys­ tems [163,164]. In 2020, Zhang et al. utilized an azo ligand, azobenzene 4,4-dicarboxylic acid (Azo-DCA), to construct hypoxia-responsive CuMOFs (Fig. 11D) [71]. Ce6 was physically incorporated and the nano­ system could quickly disassociate and release $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and Ce6 for CDT and sonodynamic therapy (SDT), respectively. ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -mediated Fenton-like reactions and $^10_{2}$ generated by sonication of Ce6 worked together to significantly elevate the ROS level (Fig. 11E). Importantly, nanoscale MOFs rapidly degraded and efficiently penetrated tumor tissues, demonstrating a remarkable inhibition of MCF-7 tumor under ultrasonic irradiation. By using the same Cu-MOFs, Sun et al. developed an AIPHloaded Cu-MOF for orthotopic pancreatic carcinoma treatment [160]. Under the hypoxia condition, ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and AIPH were significantly released. Upon ultrasonic irradiation, ${\bf N}_{2}$ bubbles and radicals were generated by AIPH and the former enhanced the penetration of nano­ particles. A synergy between ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -mediated CDT and SDT gave rise to efficient inhibition of pancreatic carcinoma.
3.1.4.4. Photo-sensitive ligands. Photosensitizers that can absorb light energy to generate $^10_{2}$ in the presence of molecular oxygen are good ligands and have been extensively investigated in different MOFs. The PS-based nanoMOFs have been seen as the fourth generation PSs because of the porous, periodic nanostructure that not only avoid un­ wanted aggregation of PSs but also provide channels for the efficient diffusion of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and the generated $^10_{2}$ [165,166]. Porphyrin-based li­ gands such as $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{L}$ [120] and $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP}$ [121] have been used for designing Cu-MOFs in cancer therapy. TCPP is the mostly used porphyrins in CuMOFs because of the facile preparation of ultrathin nanosheets, whose thickness was about 1 to $5\mathrm{nm}$ by different research groups. Besides of the above-mentioned photothermal effect [52] and $^10_{2}$ generation by a Russell mechanism in the dark condition [53], the intrinsic PDT of TCPP dictated that Cu-TCPP NSs could be used for PDT of cancer. In addition, the two-dimensional structure makes it an excellent carrier of chemo­ drugs; for example, a high DOX loading content of $33\%$ could be realized by the $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between NSs and DOX, which enabled the chemo-photodynamic therapy of cancer [67]. In addition, the GSHdepleting ability of Cu-MOFs further enhanced the chemophotodynamic therapy effect of Cu-TCPP NSs.
## 3.2. Antibacterial
Bacterial infections are the main threat to public health and devel­ oping novel antibacterial agents that outperform or work synergistically with antibiotics has become a hot spot [167]. The use of metals as antibacterial agents could date back to antiquity [168,169], a history much longer than those of antibiotics [170], antimicrobial peptides [171], inorganic nonmetallic nanomaterials [172], and synthetic anti­ bacterial polymers [173] that emerged since last century. The antibac­ terial effect of transition metal ions is in the order: $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}>\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{2+}>\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{2+}\geq$ $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}\geq\mathsf{Z n}^{2+}\geq\mathsf{C u}^{2+}\geq\mathsf{F e}^{2+}>\mathsf{M n}^{2+}>\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ , as provided by Antibac­ terial and Antifungal Society of Japan [174,175]. Among these metal ions, ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ displays a moderate antibacterial effect.
The intrinsic antibacterial activity of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ renders $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs an antibacterial platform either alone or in combination with other func­ tional ligands and antibacterial composites. In this section, the recent progresses of antibacterial Cu-MOFs were introduced from three aspects: pure MOFs, MOF-coated composites, and MOF-embedded composites (Table 2).
## 3.2.1. Pure Cu-MOFs
Identifying the antibacterial performance and mechanisms of pure MOF is essential to design advanced MOF composites with improved bacterial inhibition effect. Shams et al. found that pure HKUST-1 dis­ played a notable antibacterial effect against E. coli and S. aureus by significantly disrupting the cell membrane, discharge cell constituents, and inhibiting DNA synthesis [180]. The size and morphology of the MOFs were not reported but it showed MICs of 75 and $150~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ against S. aureus and E. coli, respectively. As shown in Fig. 12A, sunken S. aureus were observed when treated with HKUST-1 at a concentration of $1\times\mathrm{MIC}$ and more severe membrane damage at $2\times\mathrm{MIC}$ . Fan et al. synthesized needle-like Cu-MOF-74 with a grain size of $3{\mathrm{-}}4.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ by a simple hydrothermal method and explored its potential in cyanobacteria inhibition (Fig. 12B) [178]. The inhibition effect of Cu-MOF-74 on Microcystis aeruginosa was highly concentration-dependent, where low concentration MOFs promoted growth while high concentration led to inhibition. The inhibitory mechanisms of Cu-MOF-74 include release of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , agglomeration, ROS (including •OH) production, and oxidative damage.
Cytosine or guanine-incorporated BioMOFs (main ligand: 4-4, biphenyldicarboxylic acid), was facilely prepared by one-step sono­ chemical method [176,177]. They displayed a moderate bacterial in­ hibition (MICs: $1.6{-}1.8\mathrm{mg/mL})$ by releasing $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and biological ligands, where cytosine and guanine could modulate the expression of rsbA gene in Proteus mirabilis and oprD gene in Pseudomonas aeruginosa, respectively.
Jo and coworkers verified the excellent antibacterial activities of a series of micron-sized Cu-MOFs containing glutarates and four different bipyridyl ligands (bpy, bpa, bpe, and bpp) [179]. The four bipyridyl ligands scarcely affected the antibacterial effects of Cu-MOFs and they exhibited an MBC of $20~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ against five tested strains (Table 1), a value much lower than those of other reported pure Cu-MOFs. These MOFs exhibited good stability in aqueous solution and a slow ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ release, where the maximum released copper concentration of $29\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ was observed at $48\mathrm{h}$ . Therefore, the antibacterial activity was probably attributed to the interactions between bacteria and metal ions on MOF surfaces.
Overall, the antibacterial activity of these Cu-MOFs is predominantly determined by the release of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ (and the bioactive ligands). A comprehensive investigation of the impact of the size and morphology of Cu-MOFs on the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ release rate and the antibacterial effect could be further performed.
## 3.2.2. MOF-coated antibacterial surfaces
Cu-MOFs can be coated on the surfaces of fibers (wood [189], bamboo [189], wool [190], and cotton [191]), film [192,193], mem­ branes [194], and nanoparticles [195] to contrust versatile antibacterial surfaces or coatings. There are three ways to realize Cu-MOF surface modification: in-situ synthesis [194], layer-by-layer growth [188], and in-situ deposition [189]. The efficient surface modification highly relies on the intense interactions between surface and MOF; therefore, abun­ dant surficial functional groups, such as carboxylic groups, are condu­ cive to the MOF coating and growth. For example, polysachharide-based fibers like cotton and cellulose were first treated with carbox­ ymethylation prior to the MOF modification [193].
The in-situ synthesis method involves a one-pot synthesis of a mixture containing ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , organic ligands, and the substrates, where the addition sequences play a key role in the modification efficiency [191]. In 2019, Ren et al. reported a HKUST-1-chitosan composite film by a two-step synthesis, where cupric ions were firsted immobilized on the naturally antibacterial polymer chitosan by freeze-drying and the formed film were then treated with $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ solution [196]. The com­ posite fulfilled slow $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ release and thereby reduced cytotoxicity. Interestingly, the composite possessed a stronger inhibition in S. aureus than E. coli, which was possibly due to the more negatively charged surface of S. aureus. In the in vivo infected wound healing study, the composite film not only killed bacteria but also promoted vessel regeneration.
For the layer-by-layer technique, the substrates are alternately treated with copper- and ligand-containing solution for many cycles. In 2018, Rubin et al. prepared Cu-MOFs (ligand: $\mathrm{NH_{2}H_{3}B T C})$ -functional­ ized cotton by this method and a total of 30 layers of MOFs with a size of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ocetahedral crystals were coated on the cotton surfaces (Fig. 13A and B) [183]. The post-synthetic modification of Cu-MOFs by valeric anhydride slowed down the $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ release in the first 6 h while did not comprosmise the anitbacterial effects. The resulting composites slowly released $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and induced a 3- and 4-log reduction of E. coli in nutrient broth media after a 6- and $24{\cdot}\mathrm{h}$ treatment, respectively (Fig. 13C). Furthermore, the modified cotton inhibit bacterial attach­ ment under both dry and wet codnitions, suggesting a potential appli­ cation in antibacterial and antifouling textile. This method precisely controls the cycle numbers but is more time- and solvent-consuming than in-situ synthesis.
## 3.2.3. $\boldsymbol{C u}$ -MOFs-embedded composites
Cu-MOFs-embedded antibacterial fiber and hydrogels could be readily prepared by electrospining and crosslinking technique using a mixture of MOF and polymer. Polymers including PVA [192,197], pectin [79], PEG [118], and poly(lactic acid) (PLA) [78], polysiloxanes [198] have been employed to construct antibacterial Cu-MOF-polymer composites.
Liu and coworkers prepared a flexible fibrous composite membrane of sphere-like Cu-citric acid MOF $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in diameter) and PLA by elec­ trospining [78]. The as-prepared Cu-MOF-1/PLA fibrous membrane showed outstanding antibacterial activity, elciting enhanced inhibitory rates than the parent Cu-MOF-1 at the same concentration of $250~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ $(99.4\%$ vs. $93.9\%$ inhibition in E. coli; $99.6\%$ vs. $97.9\%$ in S. aureus). The mechanism study suggested that the slow release of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ destroyed the integrity and permeability of cell membrane and induced enzyme inactivation. Gwon et al. designed Cu-, Co-, and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF-based phot­ crosslinked hydrogels by UV light-triggered crosslinking of PEG-DA and 4-arm-PEG-SH [186]. The antibacterial performances of these hydrogels in E. coli and S. aureus were in the order of : $\mathrm{Cu}>\mathrm{Co}>\mathrm{Zn}$ MOFs. Impotantly, Cu-MOF gel showed no cytotoxicity in human dermal fi­ broblasts yet induced a $99.9\%$ bacterial inhibition at MBC $(20~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ , indicating a potency in skin disease and wound treatment.
Since porous MOFs are excellent carriers of small molecules, CuMOFs can be leveraged to load other antibacterial agents to fulfill combinational bacteria inhibition. Yu et al. prepared CuS NPs incorpo­ rated HKUST-1 by a simple in-situ sulfuration for antibacterial appli­ cation (Fig. 14A) [199]. CuS NPs have both photodynamic and photothermal effects under near-infrared light irradiation for enhanced antibacterial performance. In this study, CuS@HKUST-1 combines the traditional light-responsiveness of CuS NPs with the advantages of the flexible carrier HKUST-1, presenting an effective new drug for rapid antibacterial therapy. After NIR light irradiation for $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the com­ posite induced inhibition rates of $99.70\%$ and $99.80\%$ against S. aureus and E. coli, respectively.
In addition, the catalytic effect of Cu-MOFs could be harnessed to construct antibacterial composites. In 2019, Liu et al. developed a CuMOF/GOx hybrid nanocatalyst for a self-activated cascade reagent for bacterial inhibition (Fig. 14B) [59]. The peroxidase-like activity of CuTCPP(Fe) nanosheets can transform $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which was in situ catalyti­ cally generated by GOx-mediated glucose oxidation, into antibacterial OH. Glucose plus composites significantly reduced the viability of E. coli and S. aureus relative to glucose+ $\mathrm{-H_{2}O_{2}}$ (Fig. 14C). The continuous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ supplying by the glucose oxidation not only provided OH gen­ eration but also minimized adverse effects of direct use of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [59]. Similarly, Cheng et al. developed a $G0\mathbf{x}$ and L-arginine co-loaded CuMOF (CuBDC) nanocomposite to kill bacteria by self-enhanced doublecascade reaction [200]. The GOx catalytically oxidized glucose to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ which subsequently triggered the production of a large amount of oxygen radicals, ONOO , and NO to kill bacteria. The com­ posite displayed a higher inhibition efficiency against S. aureus, where
$97\%S.$ . aureus inactivation was achieved at a concentration of $3.8\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ while $38~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ was needed for $98\%$ inactivation of $E$ . coli.
## 3.3. Biosensor
Detection of important biological molecules and pathogens of in­ terest (including some cancer cells, bacteria, and virus) is conducive to diagnosing and monitoring various diseases. Different nanomaterials including carbon materials, metal oxides, and metal nanoparticles are employed for fabricating biosensors [201]. Nanoscale MOFs display enhanced performances in terms of sensitivity, selectivity, detection speed, and simplicity owing to the high surface area and uniform pore and channel sizes. In addition, excellent thermal and mechanical sta­ bility enable detections in harsh conditions. More importantly, MOFs are emerging as a promising electrochemical sensing candidate due to the high mass transfer capacities [202]. Intriguingly, some Cu-MOFs with intrinsically peroxidase- or oxidase-like activity render them excellent biosensors in harsh conditions that natural enzymes cannot work [203]. As summarized in Table 3, Cu-MOFs have been extensively explored as sensors for various small molecules $\mathrm{(H_{2}O_{2}}$ [204–223], glucose [224–246], dopamine [91,247–253], signal molecules, glutathione [254,255], drugs [256–261] etc. [262]), macromolecules (proteins [263–266], lipopolysaccharide [267], DNA [65,204], mRNA [268–271], etc.) and microbes (E. coli [272,273] and S. aureus [274]).
## 3.3.1. $H_{2}O_{2}$ detection
$\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , a type of ROS, participates in various metabolisms in the human body and acts as a signal transduction molecule for various biological processes. Aberrant levels of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ may cause a variety of diseases, such as Alzheimer's disease, cancer, and cardiovascular dis­ eases [279–281]. The currently reported methods for detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ include chromatography [282], positron emission tomography [283], colorimetry [284] and electrochemical methods [285], etc. Of these, the electrochemical method is most used because of the advantages of high sensitivity, low cost, high stability, and simple operation. Biological enzyme-based electrochemical sensors are difficult to prepare and the life span is short, which limits their applications. Therefore, developing highly effective non-enzymatic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensors is of great importance. The poor electrical conductivity and electrocatalytic performance of CuMOFs dictate that a combination of Cu-MOFs and other materials that have excellent electrical conductivity and catalytic effect could be promising in electrochemistry applications [206]. Various materials including reduced carbon nanotubes [210], electrochemically reduced graphene oxide (ERGO) [206,215], acetylene black [213], gold NPs [205], silver NPs [211], PtCu alloy , Hemin and G-quadruplex-hemin DNAzyme [204,207,217], MXene nanosheets [208], and $\mathsf{C u}_{5.4}\mathrm{O}$ NPs [212], have been employed to prepare $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs-based composites for enzyme-free electrochemical detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .
In 2017, Li et al. developed a hybrid of Cu-MOF (ligand: 2,4,6-tris (3,5-dicarboxylphenylamino)-1,3,5-triazine, $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{TDPAT)}$ and nanosized ERGO for efficient linear detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in a wide range from 4 to $12000~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ with a detection limit of $0.17~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Fig. 15A) [215]. The composite suspension was coated on the surface of glass carbon elec­ trode (GCE) for glucose detection. In this system, Cu-TDPAT MOFs provided not only open metal sites but also Lewis basic sites for bio­ mimetic catalysis and adsorption affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [215]. The $\pi{-}\pi$ packing and hydrogen bonding interactions between Cu-TPDAT and ERGO improved the charge transport, making the composite a sensitive and selective sensor for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The detection assay in diluted human serum and urine demonstrated a good recovery ranging from $97.68\%$ to $103.2\%$ .
In 2019, Bai et al. constructed a composite of single-walled CNT and Cu-TCPP two-dimensional nanosheets and pushed the detection limit to a ultralow level of ${\mathsf{5}}{\mathrm{~nM}}$ [210]. The composite nanofilms displayed a thickness of $2\mathrm{nm}$ and avoided the ease of aggregation of 2D nanosheets, leading to an enhanced electrical conductivity. In addition, the CuTCPP/CNT composite showed much lower detection limit than the Co and Ni-TCPP/CNT and Cu-TCPP/GO counterparts.
Recently, a composite of Cu-TCPP and G-quadruplex-hemin (G4- hemin) was prepared by Ma et al. for amplified electrochemical $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensing (Fig. 15B) [207]. G4-hemin is a complex of G-quadruplex DNA and hemin displaying strong peroxidase-like activity; its stability was improved by using Cu-TCPP as a nanocarrier. The Cu-TCPP nanofilm, prepared by a surfactant-assisted method, showed a wrinkled morphology, and offered high surface area for active sites of substrates. The complex was then coated on GCE by the Langmuir-Schafer method and a low $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection limit of $0.03~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ was realized by signal amplification.
Direct detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in blood or culture medium is of great po­ tential in practical applications. Cui et al. developed Cu-MOFs (ligand: $m$ -phthalic acid)/Hemin composite for electrochemical sensing of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in blood or released from living cells [217]. The nanocomposites showed a micron-sized 2D lamellar structure and excellent electrocatalytic performances. The electrochemical sensor could linearly detect $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in a wide range $\left(0.01{-}5.0\ \mathrm{mM}\right)$ . Furthermore, the precise detection in biological samples were verified by detecting the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ levels in human serum samples from normal and cancer patients and monitoring the realtime $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ release amount from HepG2 cancer cells.
## 3.3.2. Glucose detection
Diabetes is a complex, chronic metabolic disease characterized by hyperglycemia, which can lead to a variety of acute and chronic com­ plications, including renal failure and cardiovascular and cerebrovas­ cular diseases in severe cases [286–289]. Rapid and precise detection of glucose is urgently needed for diagnose and monitor the disease.
The electrochemical sensor is also used for glucose detection owing to the high sensitivity and selectivity. In 2019, Wu et al. reported a multi-layer composite films of Cu-MOF (ligand: $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})$ and multiwalled CNTs (MWNTs) for glucose sensing (Fig. 16A) [227]. The multilayer films were prepared by electrodeposition on GCE for several cycles. The resulting eight-layer Cu-MOF/MWNTs exhibited optimal oxidation effect of glucose in strong alkaline condition $(0.1\mathrm{~M~NaOH})$ , showing a wide linear range of $0.5\upmu\mathrm{M}-11.84\up m\mathrm{M}$ and a detection limit of $0.4\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ . The strong alkaline condition could somehow limit the further detection applications in practical conditions and the system could be further improved.
Glucose is the substrate of natural enzyme GOx, which oxidizes glucose into gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the presence of molecular oxy­ gen. Therefore, composites based on GOx and Cu-MOFs are expected to maintain the high sensitivity and specificity of GOx. In 2019, Cheng et al. designed an artificial multienzyme cascade system for detection of glucose, where GOx was in situ encapsulated by Cu-MOFs (ligand: $\mathrm{H_{3}B T C)}$ layer grown on three-dimensional porous conducting copper foam electrode [290]. The porous MOF architecture not only ensures the efficient diffusion of substrate and generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ but also strengthens the thermal stability of $G{\bf O}{\bf x},$ , where the enzyme activity remained about $80\%$ after treatment at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Mechanically, the immobilized GOx triggered the catalytic oxidation of glucose to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ which subsequently underwent electro-catalyzed reduction by Cu-MOF nano­ zyme/copper foam and thereby produced detectable electric signal. The complex electrode showed remarkably increased glucose sensitivity (Michaelis constant: $0.51\mathrm{\mM}$ relative to free GOx $(25.7~\mathrm{mM})$ and
GOx@MOF $\mathrm{\nabla}^{\mathrm{39}}\mathrm{mM})$ .
Similarly, composite of bimetallic Ni/Cu MOFs (ligand: HATP) and GOx based on field-effect transistor (FET) was developed for glucose sensing (Fig. 16B) [238]. The Ni/Cu-MOFs were in-situ prepared by a one-step hydrothermal method and functionalized with GOx by glutaraldehyde crosslinking. Importantly, the bimetallic MOF as channel layers displayed good field effect performance and good response to glucose, having a high sensitivity of $26.05~{\upmu\mathrm{Acm}}^{-2}~\mathrm{mM}^{-1}$ and a low detection limit of $0.51~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ .
Besides of electrochemistry method, Hu et al. designed a glucose detection method by harnessing the surface-enhanced Raman scattering of the composites of Au NPs and Cu-TCPP nanosheet MOF (Fig. 16C) [235]. In this system, an enzyme-free tandem catalysis strategy was utilized. Specifically, glucose was first catalytically oxidized by Au NPs to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which oxidized leucomalachite green with no Raman activity to malachite green with Raman activity by the peroxidase-like catalytic effect of Cu-TCPP MOFs. This composite enabled a glucose detection limit of $0.16~\mathrm{mM}$ in saliva and a good recovery ranging from $96.9\%$ to $100.8\%$ .
## 3.3.3. Other small biomolecules
Cu-MOFs have recently been used for detection of small gas mole­ cules such as NO [277] and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ [278], which are important signal molecules in biological systems [291–293]. Alizadeh et al. developed a
CuO/Cu-MOF (ligand: ${\bf C}_{6}{\bf H}_{5}{\bf N O S};$ nanocomposite for highly sensitive detection of NO (detection limit: $7.8~\mathrm{nM}\mathrm{\cdot}$ [277]. The nanocompositecoated Au electrodes displayed electrocatalytic oxidation of NO at 0.6 V. The NO released from L-arginine or $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ treated cancer cells was successfully detected for real-time NO monitoring by an electrochemical microfluidic sensor. By virtue of the superior affinity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ for ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , Zheng et al. developed a Cu/Tb-MOF for luminescent turn-on ratio­ metric detecting of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ [278]. The weak $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion emission at $554~\mathrm{nm}$ could be significantly enhanced after the binding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{u}}^{2+}$ by limiting the ligand-centered emission (Fig. 17A). The hybrid MOF showed a good relationship of NaS at concentrations lower than $1.6\:\mathrm{mM}$ and a low detection limit of $1.20~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ towards sulfide. Other analytes including cysteine, GSH, NaBr etc. showed little interfere with the se­ lective detection of $\mathrm{Na_{2}S}$ .
Dopamine is a neurotransmitter in cells and its detection was demonstrated by Cu-TCPP MOFs. Gold NPs and conductive poly(xan­ thurenic acid) were immobilized on the surface of Cu-TCPP nanosheets, and the resulting composite showed enhanced electrocatalytic perfor­ mance with a detection limit of $1.0\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [250]. In 2021, Song et al. used a two-dimensional Cu-HHTP MOF (HTTP: 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytri­ phenylene) as channel in solution-gated thin-film transistors (Fig. 17B), which showed high sensitivity and selectivity of dopamine (detection limit: $100~\mathrm{{nM}}$ by a possible oxidation mechanism on the MOF channel surface [252]. The porous channel facilitated ion doping and amplified transistor architecture function, leading to highly sensi­ tive and selective detection of dopamine.
## 3.3.4. DNA and RNA
Detection of nucleic acids is very important in identification of virus infection, especially in the context of COVID pandemic. Threedimensional Cu-MOFs (ligands: $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{CI}$ mdcpBr and dps) were developed to detect human immunodeficiency Virus 1 (HIV-1) ds-DNA and Sudan virus (SUDV) RNA [269]. The Cu-MOFs can interact with fluoresceinlabeled probe DNA by multiple interactions including electrostatic, hydrogen bonding interactions, etc., which led to fluorescence quenching by the photoinduced electron transfer process. The target DNA or RNA with complementary base pair sequences could specifically bind to the labeled nucleic acids and switched the fluorescence on. By the highly specific fluorescence recovery, detection limits could be low as 196 and $73\mathrm{\pM}$ for HIV-1 DNA and SUDV, respectively.
In 2020, Lin et al. developed a composite of electroreduced graphene oxide and Cu-MOFs (ligand: $\mathrm{H_{2}b t c a})$ for detection of Hepatitis B virus (HBV) (Fig. 18A) [271]. Probe DNA was covalently attached to Cu-MOF at a high DNA density of $9.48~\times~10^{13}$ DNA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The composites deposited on GCE displayed a broad linear range $(50.0\mathrm{fM}$ to $10.0\ \mathrm{nM})$ and a very low detection limit (5.2 fM), suggesting a higher sensitivity than the previously reported HBV DNA biosensors (Fig. 18B) [271]. Detection of HBV DNA in human serum and urine conditions $(5{-}20\ \mathbf{pM})$ further demonstrated a good recovery of more than $95\%$ .
## 3.3.5. Microbial detection
Pathogens can cause food and water borne diseases and wound in­ fections. Therefore, precise detection is essential for rapid identifying the cause of diseases. Wang and coworkers reported a sensitive colori­ metric detection of S. aureus, a typical Gram-positive bacterium, by CuMOFs (ligand: 2-aminoterephthalic acid) (Fig. 19A) [274]. The CuMOFs with a uniform size of $550~\mathrm{nm}$ were prepared by a mixed sol­ vothermal method and covalently functionalized with S. aureus aptamer for sensitive and selective recognition of S. aureus. The homogeneous size and morphology of Cu-MOFs offer a prerequisite that each bacterial would nearly link the same number of NPs, which is key to the quanti­ tative detection of S. aureus. After the magnetic separation of S. aureus bounded Cu-MOF, the resultant Cu-MOF NPs could be measured by a colorimetric analysis of $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), where MOF exerts the peroxidase-like activity to oxidize TMB and induce a yellow chromogenic reaction in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Fig. 19B). A linear relationship was obtained at a S. aureus concentration range of 50 to 10000 CFU $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Fig. 19C) and the detection limit was $20\mathrm{CFU}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . This kind of aptamer-functionalized MOF holds a potential in accurately detecting the pathogens of interest.
Similarly, E. coli, a kind of typical Gram-negative bacterium, was successfully detected by aptamer-modified Cu-MOFs (ligand: 2-amino­ terephthalic acid) [273]. The intrinsic peroxidase-like activity of CuMOFs fulfilled TMB-based colorimetric analysis of $E$ . coli with a detec­ tion limit of $2\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ and a linear range from $16{-}1.6\times10^{6}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ . The selective detection of $E.$ coli O157:H7 was established by comparing the detections of other strains including $E$ . coli 25922, E. coli ETEC, S. aureus, etc.
Instead of the colorimetric detection, electrochemical biosensing of E. coli was recently reported by Gupta and coworkers [272]. Cu-BTC MOFs were mixed with conducting polymer polyaniline to construct the electrochemically active platform, which was further modified with anti-E. coli antibodies as an electrochemical immunosensor for E. coli. By virtue of the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy, a low detection limit of 2 CFU/mL and a short response time of $2\:\mathrm{min}$ was obtained.
## 3.4. Biocatalysis
Copper is a very important transition metal catalyst (copper-cata­ lyzed azide-alkyne click chemistry [294], for example) and acts as a cofactor in many natural enzymes [295]. Therefore, it is no surprise that Cu-MOFs could be employed for catalysis considering the advantages of MOF-based catalysts such as high internal surface areas and active site densities [296]. In this section, we mainly introduce the applications of Cu-MOFs in the catalysis reactions involved with biomolecules, cells, and organisms.
Some Cu-MOFs per se have peroxidase- or protease-like activity [59,274,297]. Besides of the applications in cancer treatment and bio­ sensing that was described in the previous sections, recent studies sug­ gest that some Cu-MOFs can act as a catalyst to accelerate the NO generation from S-nitrosothiols in blood [76,298,299]. Zhang et al. designed a Cu-MOF (ligand: $\mathrm{H_{3}B T C)}$ -incorporated vascular graft for catalytic generation of NO that is capable of accelerating the formation an intact endothelial monolayer [76]. The Cu-MOFs were entrapped in poly(ε-caprolactone) (PCL) fibers for enhancing the MOF stability in serum and long-term NO producing ability. Garren et al. recently designed composites of Cu-MOFs (ligand: $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTTri})$ and NO donor (Snitroso-N-acetyl-penicillamine) for controlled release of NO and bacte­ rial inhibition (Fig. 20A and B) [300]. NO was generated by the catalytic decomposition of NO donor, which fulfilled a controlled release of NO within $24\mathrm{h}$ by tuning the weight percentages of the Cu-MOFs $(1\%,3\%$ , and $5\%$ ) in the composites. The higher weight ratio of Cu-MOFs, the faster and more NO was released (Fig. 20C and D). In the in vitro anti­ bacterial study, the composites displayed a $99\%$ and $95\%$ reduction in adhered and planktonic MRSA, respectively.
Cu-MOFs could be used for catalytic synthesis of drugs in organic solvents and even in cells and animals. For example, HKUST-1 was employed to catalyze the synthesis of quinazolinones, which are nitrogen-containing heterocyclic molecules with various therapeutic functions [301]. HKUST-1 showed a high yield of about $98\%$ at $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ in the ethanol reflux condition, much more efficient than the inorganic Cu compounds and other MOFs. Interestingly, Wang et al. performed a copper-catalyzed azide–alkyne click synthesis of drug in mitochondria by using a mitochondrial targeting copper-embedded $z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF (ligand: $\mathrm{NH_{2}B D C)}$ (Fig. 21A) [302]. The triazole drug formation was demon­ strated in human breast cancer MCF-7 cells and animal model Caeno­ rhabditis elegans (Fig. 21B and C). Similar mitochondrial-targeting AIE photosensitizer was synthesized by the assistance of F-127 stabilized HKUST-1 NPs (Fig. 21D) [303]. In this design, ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ in MOF could be reduced to $\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ by intracellular GSH, which catalyzed the azide–alkyne click synthesis of mitochondrial-targeting photosensitizer TPATrzPy- $^{.3+}$ for specific PDT of HeLa cancer cells and liver tumors in zebrafish (Fig. 21E and F).
Besides of the intrinsic catalytic activity of Cu-MOFs, the immobili­ zation of natural enzymes by Cu-MOFs can also be used for biological catalysis and cascade reactions [59]. The porous structures of MOFs provide the efficient substance diffusion channel but also improve the stability and catalytic activity of enzymes [304–306]. For example, Li et al. designed Cytochrome c (Cyt c)-incorporated Cu-BDC MOF for enhanced peroxidase activity and stability [305]. The bio-composite displayed a 12-fold catalytic efficiency improvement relative to Cyt c and enhanced enzyme stability to trypsin and thermal treatment. $\alpha$ -Glucosidase and GOx were incorporated into Cu-MOF (ligand: $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - bipyridine) for screening of $\alpha$ -glucosidase inhibitor and discovering antidiabetic drugs [307]. $\upbeta$ -Glucosidase was immobilized into Cu-PABA MOFs for enhanced hydrolysis of cellulose and high enzyme reusability, where about $90\%$ activity remained after 10 cycles of hydrolysis of carboxymethylcellulose [308].
In contrast to improve the enzyme activity, Xu et al. found that twodimensional $\mathrm{Cu(bpy)_{2}(O T f)_{2}}$ MOF can inhibit the activity of $\alpha$ -chymo­ trypsin (ChT) but $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(b i m)_{4}}$ cannot [309]. The molecular mechanisms underlying the enzyme inhibition was attributed to the irreversible co­ ordination interactions between $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and His-57 in ChT. The modulable enzyme activity by MOFs could be used for controlling the cellular processes.
## 3.5. Wound healing
Recently, MOFs have attracted increasing attentions in wound healing [310]. The first exploration of Cu-MOFs in wound healing was made by Xiao et al. in 2017, who found that HKUST-1 NPs in poly (polyethyleneglycol citrate-co-N-isopropylacrylamide) hydrogel enhanced wound healing in diabetes [311]. Although $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ have been reported to induce angiogenesis, the rapid release could lead to cyto­ toxicity. The hydrogel protected the rapid degradation of HKUST-1 NPs from protein solution and facilitated slow release of cupric ions that is pivotal to the reduced toxicity and enhanced dermal cell migration and wound closure. In the in vivo splinted excisional dermal wound diabetic mouse model, the system fulfilled angiogenesis, collogen deposition, and re-epithelialization. In 2018, the same group further demonstrated the folic acid-modified HKUST-1 NPs could slowly release $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and enhance the wound healing in diabetes [312].
NO promotes wound healing and the controllable release is para­ mount to fulfill the biological functions. Zhang et al. constructed a NOreleasing coaxial nanofiber scaffolds by electrospinning, which sus­ tainably released NO at a rate of $\boldsymbol{1.74\mathrm{nmol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ by incorporating NOcontaining HKUST-1 nanoparticle into the core layer of the fibers (Fig. 22A) [77]. The controllably released ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{u}}^{2+}$ and NO synergistically improve the wound healing in a diabetic wound model (Fig. 22B). Yao et al. reported a photothermal-responsive NO release platform based on porous MOF microneedle array patch (Fig. 22C) [313]. The photo­ thermal effect of graphene oxide triggered the rapid release of NO. Meanwhile, the microneedle array rendered the precise and deep-tissue delivery of NO, establishing the accelerated vascularization, collagen deposition, and tissue regeneration in a diabetic rat model (Fig. 22D).
Microbial infections are associated with wound treatment, which urges the multifunctional materials that could not only improve wound healing but also kill the pathogens [196,197,314]. For example, Cuniacin MOF have been explored in wound healing by harnessing the antibacterial and angiogenesis effect of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and the angiogenesis and hemangiectasis of niacin [74,315]. Chen et al. designed Cu-MOF incorporated microcapsules by microfluidic electrospray technology [315]. The smart microcapsule systems possessed a bacteriaresponsively degradable alginate shell and a $\mathrm{{Cu/Zn}}$ MOF core, which enabled the programmable disintegration to release ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and niacin in response to different degrees of infections. Remarkably accelerated chronic wound healing was established in an infected full-thickness skin defect model. In 2021, Wang et al. developed a composite of copperniacin MOF and gelatin methacrylate (GelMA) hydrogel for delivery of basic fibroblast growth factor and wound healing (Fig. 23) [74]. The composite exhibited excellent mechanical properties, biocompatibility, and antibacterial activity. In addition, in the full-thickness defect model of skin wound, acceleareted wound healing was realized by suppressing the inflammatory responses and enhancing the new blood vessel for­ mation and collagen deposition. Cu-MOFs-embedded hydrogel repre­ sents a class of wound dressing of great potential owing to the multiple bioactivities of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ .
Liu et al. designed a Cu-TCPP nanosheet/GOx hybrid nanocatalyst for self-activated antibacterial activity and wound healing [59]. In this system, local glucose was catalytically oxidized to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ that was further converted into bactericidal OH by the peroxidase-like ac­ tivity of Cu-TCPP nanosheets.
Photo-triggered antibacterial systems are also attractive in wound healing owing to the spatiotemporal control and the ROS-based PDT or hyperthermia-based PTT. By using photosensitizer porphyrin as organic ligands, Han and coworkers found that Cu-doped PCN 244 (metal: Zr6 clusters; ligand: porphyrin) possessed enhanced photocatalytic activity and photothermal effects for efficient killing bacteria and wound healing [316]. The MOF doped with $10\%\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ displayed the best antibacterial effect, where $99.71\%$ inhibition of S. aureus rate was realized upon irradiation of $660~\mathrm{{nm}}$ light for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The in vivo S. aureus infection model further evidenced the bacterial eradication and faster wound healing by the released ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ .
## 4. Conclusions and outlook
In summary, the synthesis and biomedical applications of Cu-MOFs and their composites are reviewed. The physiochemical properties of Cu and the porous structures have made Cu-MOFs attractive in various applications [317]. In the anticancer applications, Cu-MOFs could scavenge intracellular antioxidant substances like GSH and disrupt the redox hemostasis by a Fenton-like reaction [318]. By using electro­ chemical and Raman spectroscopic techniques, Cu-MOFs have proved a broad range of substrates with high selectivity and sensitivity even in biological samples. Although some encouraging antibacterial results were obtained, the research is still in the early stage and the efficacy in eradicating biofilms, a community of bacteria with organized structures that are apt to induce resistance to antibiotics and account for over $80\%$ microbial infections in the body, [319] is rarely explored. On one hand, Cu-MOFs have expanded the drug synthesis conditions from organic solvents and high temperature to a mild temperature and more sophis­ ticated conditions in the cell and animal levels; on the other hand, the catalytic activity of Cu-MOFs in turn promotes the applications in bio­ sensing and disease treatment. The intrinsic angiogenesis and antibac­ terial activity of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ make Cu-MOFs promising in wound healing. For example, the combination of Cu-MOFs and hydrogels is expected to construct functional wound dressings with great translation potential.
Despite the explosive proceedings of Cu-MOFs in diverse biomedical applications, there are still several issues needed to pay attention to:
(i) $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ release rate is an important factor in many biological ap­ plications. Accelerated release of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ at lesion sites (like tumors and infected wounds) and sustained release at normal tissues urge the dy­ namic management of $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs’ stability, where the best therapeutic effect and minimum systemic metal toxicity could be simultaneously met.
(ii) The size, morphology, and stability of Cu-MOFs are paramount in the drug delivery and biosensing scenarios. For example, MOFs with uniform size and morphology are expected to get reproducible results of in vivo biodistributions and biosensing. In addition, physiological sta­ bility of Cu-MOFs is a prerequisite to the excellent in vivo antitumor effect by overcoming multiple biological barriers.
(iii) Some biological mechanisms, like antibacterial mechanism, of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and Cu-MOFs have not been fully uncovered. Therefore, the bio­ logical and molecular level understanding of the underlying modes of action should be further explored to guide the material design and applications. This is also verified by the recent in-depth identification of the roles of copper in the oncogenesis, progression, and metastasis of cancer and the emerging copper-targeting cancer therapy.
(iv) Advanced Cu-MOFs could be further explored. Efforts could be made in developing hybrid metal or ligand-based MOFs [110], func­ tional ligands [320], and hierarchical MOFs that are synthesized by the guiding of destruction mechanisms [321]. For example, Cu/Fe hybrid MOFs acquire the photothermal conversion ability and could be used in photothermal ablation of tumor and bacteria.
(v) Last but not the least, Cu-MOFs-based composites are a great arsenal of functional materials. For example, synergistic antibacterial or anticancer effects could be obtained in the composite; the combination of Cu-MOFs with electrical conducting materials could significantly improve the detection sensitivity in biosensing. As an important branch of MOF-based composite, MOF-polymer composites could be elegantly prepared by various methods including grafting polymers from and to MOFs, modulating MOFs growth by polymers, and so on [72]. These tailor-made MOF-polymer composites could own various hybrid archi­ tectures and may facilitate better biomedical applications and even for commercial applications. More importantly, these materials may find applications beyond biomedical fields, which could be expanded to environment [322–326], energy [327], and food safety [328] fields.
